{
  "sn47.43:0.1": "Linked Discourses 47.43 ",
  "sn47.43:0.2": "5. Freedom From Death ",
  "sn47.43:0.3": "The Path ",
  "sn47.43:1.1": "At Sāvatthī. ",
  "sn47.43:1.2": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: ",
  "sn47.43:1.3": "“Mendicants, this one time, when I was first awakened, I was staying in Uruvelā at the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. ",
  "sn47.43:1.4": "As I was in private retreat this thought came to mind: ",
  "sn47.43:1.5": "‘The four kinds of mindfulness meditation are the path to convergence. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment. ",
  "sn47.43:2.1": "What four? ",
  "sn47.43:2.2": "A mendicant would meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "sn47.43:2.3": "Or they’d meditate observing an aspect of feelings … ",
  "sn47.43:2.4": "or mind … ",
  "sn47.43:2.5": "or principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "sn47.43:2.6": "The four kinds of mindfulness meditation are the path to convergence. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment.’ ",
  "sn47.43:3.1": "And then the divinity Sahampati, knowing my train of thought, as easily as a strong person would extend or con